WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.040
Let’s talk about anger

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.040
and pain in America.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.800
By now we all know what happened.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.880
George Floyd — an unarmed Black man

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.880
died after a white police officer kneeled on his neck.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.680
For more than 8 minutes.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.080
We’ve been here before

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:37.960
many, many times.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.760
So how did we get here? 

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.920
What’s behind that feeling of injustice?

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.880
Why are so many Black Americans

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.400
being killed by police?

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.680
And what has to change to make it stop?

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:08.040
This is about a lot more than one man’s death.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.760
Or the deaths of so many other

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.600
unarmed Black people before George Floyd.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.000
For the people protesting

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.560
this is about what it means to be Black in America.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.480
The roots of that inequality run deep.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:03.200
Slavery has been outlawed in America

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.280
for more than 150 years.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.800
But that didn’t give Black people

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:09.200
equal opportunities or rights.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:10.960
And it didn’t end the terror.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.440
Between 1877 and 1950

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.720
there were more than 4,000 lynchings.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:24.800
Racial segregation was only outlawed in 1964.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.680
Black Americans were only guaranteed the right to vote

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:28.920
a year later.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.120
Being Black came at a price then

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.360
and activists say it still does today.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:37.680
And police violence against Black Americans

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.520
And then you look at the numbers.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.840
Black Americans are a minority.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.280
Just 13% of the population.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:28.120
But in prison they make up 33% of all the inmates.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.240
And if you’re an unarmed Black civilian

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.560
you are more than twice as likely as a white person

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:33.520
to be killed.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.360
And police violence against Black Americans

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.040
well that happens a lot.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:39.240
Rodney King was unarmed

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.960
when he was beaten by LA police

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:42.600
and left with brain damage.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.320
Tamir Rice was playing with a toy gun in Cleveland

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:48.960
when he was shot by a police officer — 30 metres away.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:50.680
Tamir was 12 when he died.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:52.960
And Breonna Taylor — shot nine times

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:54.880
inside her own home in Louisville

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.520
by police executing a search warrant.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:10.640
And it’s not just the police doing the killing.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.200
Trayvon Martin was 17, unarmed

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.880
and killed by George Zimmerman

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.800
a neighbourhood watch co-ordinator in Florida.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:25.400
The backlash to Zimmerman’s acquittal

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:27.120
gave rise to Black Lives Matter.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.040
Ahmaud Arbery was out jogging in Georgia

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:36.040
when he was killed.

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.680
Three white men have been charged.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.040
So why does it keep happening?

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:51.360
Why do so many Black people feel that law enforcement

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.280
and the justice system are failing them?

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:56.560
Let’s look at the broken window theory. 

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.560
It was embraced by some police forces in the ’90s.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:01.160
And the idea is that if you crack down

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:04.360
on the small things you prevent bigger crimes.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:06.480
Now there are lots of studies that question

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.680
whether it actually works.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:10.520
But what we do know is that it’s led to

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:12.840
more policing of poor neighbourhoods.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.440
More police confrontations with minorities

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:18.120
more stopping, frisking and arrests of Black people

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:21.920
for things like graffiti, petty theft and drugs.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:48.920
And then there’s the practice of sending officers

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:51.000
to police communities they’re not from.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.200
No one, though, is saying policing isn’t tough.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:08.520
The issue is that there’s a line between

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.640
keeping officers safe and the abuse of power.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:13.960
They’re often trained to react quickly

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:15.320
maybe with force.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.960
But rights activists say it can be hard

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:28.800
to hold officers accountable — for lots of reasons.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.240
Police departments often investigate their own

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:32.640
when there’s a complaint.

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:47.160
Even if a case goes to trial the law says

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.000
“use of force must be judged

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.280
from the perspective of a reasonable officer”.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:03.720
According to one advocacy group

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.280
only 1% of all killings by police

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:09.080
ever lead to an officer being charged with a crime.

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:11.320
That’s charged — not convicted.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:12.680
Here are some more names.

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:14.960
NYPD officer Daniel Pantaleo

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:17.720
put Eric Garner in a chokehold that killed him.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:27.040
He was never convicted of a crime but he was fired

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:28.600
five years later.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:30.840
Police officer Darren Wilson shot and killed

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:33.720
18-year-old Michael Brown in Ferguson, Missouri.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:36.560
He was eventually cleared of criminal charges.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:39.120
“You’re threatening to drag me out of my own car?”

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:40.520
“Get out of the car!”

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:41.960
“And then you’re going to stun me?”

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:43.880
“Get out now!”

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:46.000
It’s why so many Black Americans

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.480
have been using their phones

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:49.680
to keep a record of what happened.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:07.640
In the case of George Floyd several people recorded

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:09.880
the actions of those four police officers.

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:14.480
It took a while but all four were fired and charged.

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:25.120
So what is the way forward?

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:27.840
Well, many advocates are pushing for reforms.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.640
Like getting the police to keep a record

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:31.720
of when they threaten civilians with a gun.

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:44.080
But change has been slow.

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:11.040
It’s what Colin Kaepernick was trying to say

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:12.400
when he took a knee.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:15.200
It was a peaceful protest during the national anthem

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:17.120
and he was blacklisted for it.

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:30.960
Four years later people are still protesting

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:32.720
and people are still dying.

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:35.560
And right now there’s a lot going on in America.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:38.560
Every so often there’s a new video circulating online

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:41.280
of white people calling the police on Black people.

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:42.840
“African American.”

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:44.920
In this one a woman calls 911

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:47.080
for being told to follow park rules

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:48.920
and put her dog on a leash.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:50.680
“I’m going to tell them there’s an African American man

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:51.680
threatening my life.”

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:53.320
“Please tell them whatever you like.”

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:56.240
And it’s all come to a head during a pandemic

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:58.400
where report after report points out that

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:01.120
more Black Americans are dying from the virus

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:04.080
because of things like unequal access to healthcare

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:04.760
and poverty.

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:07.000
So many people are looking for leadership

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:08.440
from the very top.

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:11.320
But President Donald Trump, well, he has plenty of critics

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:13.400
who say he’s only making things worse.

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:28.720
But they’re not just appealing to the president.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:57.320
Some people are hopeful and they’re determined

00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:59.360
to make something of this moment.

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:02.920
Because families can’t get back what they’ve lost.

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:22.320
There’s a lot going on here.

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:24.480
We’ve barely scratched the surface.

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:26.600
But the message from protesters has been

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:28.840
no justice, no peace.

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:30.840
Justice and peace.

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:33.040
That’s the future they want for themselves.

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:34.200
And for America.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:38.920
To ease some of that pain. Centuries of it.

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:42.480
That was just a primer.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:44.440
It’s what we do on Start Here.

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:47.000
But there’s, of course, a lot more to this story.

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:49.400
The George Floyd case, violence at the protests

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:51.200
and of course the politics.

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:52.880
But we’re following all of those angles

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:56.040
on aljazeera.com and on our social media pages.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:58.720
So make sure to follow, like and subscribe.

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:00.440
I’ll see you next week.

